      "expectedAnswer": "Strings can be created as primitives with single quotes, double quotes, or template literals (backticks) — all functionally equivalent for storing text — or as `String` wrapper objects via `new String(...)`, which behave differently and should be avoided.",
      "deepExplanation": "`'text'`, `\"text\"`, and `` `text` `` all produce the exact same primitive string type; the choice between quote styles is purely a style-guide preference (most teams standardize on one for consistency, e.g. Prettier defaults to single quotes). Template literals add two real capabilities beyond quoting: `${expr}` interpolation and native multi-line support without `\\n` concatenation. `new String('text')` is a trap: it creates a `String` *object* wrapping the primitive, so `typeof new String('a')` is `\"object\"` (not `\"string\"`), and `new String('a') === 'a'` is `false` even though they print the same — this is why `new String()`/`new Number()`/`new Boolean()` are considered an anti-pattern in virtually every style guide.\n\nStep 1 — Understand the topic.\nState the language/runtime rule before predicting or coding.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method.\nTrace the smallest example and identify important edge cases.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\nfunction reverse(value: string): string {\n  let result = \"\";\n  for (let i = value.length - 1; i >= 0; i -= 1) {\n    result += value[i];\n  }\n  return result;\n}\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / PRACTICAL TypeScript:\n```ts\nconst result = \"hello\".split(\"\").reverse().join(\"\");\n```\n\nStep 5 — Verify the output, complexity, and failure cases.\n\nStep 6 — Senior takeaway: explain the semantic rule and production trade-off.",
      "example": {
        "code": "const a = 'hi';\nconst b = new String('hi');\n\nconsole.log(typeof a, typeof b);\nconsole.log(a === b);\nconsole.log(a === b.valueOf());",
        "output": "\"string\" \"object\" false true",
        "explanation": "`new String('hi')` creates a wrapper *object*, not a primitive, so `typeof b` is `\"object\"` and `a === b` is `false` despite looking identical when printed. Calling `.valueOf()` unwraps the object back to the primitive, which is why that comparison succeeds — this mismatch is exactly why `new String()` is avoided in practice."
      },
      "productionExample": "```js\n// Prefer primitives + template literals; never construct with `new`.\nconst greeting = `Hello, ${user.name}!`; // primitive string, interpolated\n\n// Anti-pattern — avoid entirely:\n// const label = new String('Total'); // object wrapper, surprising equality behavior\n```",
