      "expectedAnswer": "Template literals (backtick strings, ES6) support `${expr}` interpolation and native multi-line text, and can be prefixed with a function (`tag\\`...\\``) to create a 'tagged template' that receives the literal pieces and interpolated values separately for custom processing.",
      "deepExplanation": "Anything can go inside `${}` — not just variables, but full expressions, function calls, and even nested template literals. Multi-line strings work with literal newlines inside the backticks, no `\\n` needed. Tagged templates (`` tag`Hello ${name}` ``) call `tag` with an array of the literal string segments plus the interpolated values as separate arguments — this is the mechanism behind libraries like `styled-components` (CSS-in-JS) and safe HTML-escaping helpers (`html\\`<div>${userInput}</div>\\``), letting a function control exactly how interpolated values get inserted rather than relying on plain string concatenation.\n\nStep 1 — Understand the topic.\nState the language/runtime rule before predicting or coding.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method.\nTrace the smallest example and identify important edge cases.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\n```ts\nfunction demonstrate(): string {\n  return \"Template Literals\";\n}\n\nconsole.log(demonstrate());\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / PRACTICAL TypeScript:\n```ts\nconsole.log(\"Template Literals\");\n```\n\nStep 5 — Verify the output, complexity, and failure cases.\n\nStep 6 — Senior takeaway: explain the semantic rule and production trade-off.",
      "example": {
        "code": "const name = 'Asha';\nconst items = 3;\nconsole.log(`${name} has ${items * 2} points`);\n\nfunction upper(strings, ...values) {\n  return strings.reduce((acc, str, i) => acc + str + (values[i]?.toUpperCase() ?? ''), '');\n}\nconsole.log(upper`Hello, ${name}!`);",
        "output": "\"Asha has 6 points\" \"Hello, ASHA!\"",
        "explanation": "`${items * 2}` shows any expression can be interpolated, not just a bare variable. The tagged template calls `upper` with `strings = ['Hello, ', '!']` and `values = ['Asha']`, letting the function transform the interpolated value (uppercase it) before rebuilding the final string — plain `+` concatenation can't intercept values like this."
      },
      "productionExample": "```js\n// Multi-line without \\n, and expression interpolation.\nconst email = `Hi ${user.firstName},\n\nYour order #${order.id} has shipped.\n\nThanks!`;\n\n// Tagged template for safe HTML escaping (simplified).\nfunction safeHtml(strings, ...values) {\n  return strings.reduce((acc, s, i) => acc + s + escapeHtml(values[i] ?? ''), '');\n}\n```",
