        "explanation": "`NaN === NaN` is `false` by IEEE-754 definition — `NaN` never equals anything, including itself. The global `isNaN()` coerces its argument first, so a non-numeric string like `'hello'` also reports `true`; `Number.isNaN()` is strict and only returns `true` for the actual `NaN` value, correctly returning `false` for a string."
